From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: The Playport authors <dev@playport.dev>
Date: Mon, 26 Oct 2026 17:13:21 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] audio: wake an event-driven client by how much its ring holds

The iOS driver's timer thread slept usleep(10000) between wakes: each
period was 10 ms plus the sleep's overshoot, about 11 ms on an iPhone Duo.
A client that writes one period per event then falls behind the device.
Mewgenics (480 frames per event at 48 kHz) delivered about 43,600 frames a
second for the 48,000 played ([ios_audio] ml1065 counts both channels:
870,000 a 10 s window); the ring ran dry several times a second and the
player heard it. Exact 10 ms deadlines alone brought it to 45,000: when
the client's thread runs late, two wakes fall on one wait.

Look every 2 ms to deadlines (mach_wait_until). While the ring holds less
than four periods, signal, so a client that fell behind is woken until it
has caught up; signal at least every period as before, so a client that
fills the whole ring each time is woken as it was. After a stall the next
deadline starts from now, with no burst.

Class: upstream-bug
Evidence: docs/evidence/2026-10-26-mewgenics.md
Offered-upstream: no
---
 build/ntdll-unix/audio_null_ios.c | 32 +++++++++++++++++++++++++++++--
 1 file changed, 30 insertions(+), 2 deletions(-)

diff --git a/build/ntdll-unix/audio_null_ios.c b/build/ntdll-unix/audio_null_ios.c
index b085eaa..781d6c0 100644
--- a/build/ntdll-unix/audio_null_ios.c
+++ b/build/ntdll-unix/audio_null_ios.c
@@ -982,11 +982,39 @@ static void ios_timer_loop(void *arg) {
      * event-driven client every device period so it refills the ring; exit
      * when release_stream marks the stream dead (it then joins us). */
     struct ios_stream *s = arg;
+    /* Playport: wake by how much the ring holds, on a clock that does not drift.
+     *
+     * usleep(10000) in a loop woke about every 11 ms on the phone, and a client
+     * that writes one period per event (Mewgenics: 480 frames at 48 kHz) then
+     * delivered about 43,600 frames a second for the 48,000 played; the ring ran
+     * dry several times a second and the gaps were heard. Waking to exact 10 ms
+     * deadlines still left it at 45,000: when the client's thread runs late, two
+     * wakes fall on one wait and a period is lost.
+     *
+     * So look every 2 ms, to deadlines (mach_wait_until): while the ring holds
+     * less than four periods, signal, so a client that fell behind is woken again
+     * until it has caught up; and signal at least every period, as before, so a
+     * client that fills the whole ring each time is woken as it was. */
+    mach_timebase_info_data_t tb;
+    uint64_t tick, period, next, now, last = 0, padding;
+    UINT32 period_frames = s->sample_rate / 100 ? s->sample_rate / 100 : 480;
+    mach_timebase_info(&tb);
+    tick = 2000000ull * tb.denom / tb.numer;      /* look every 2 ms */
+    period = 10000000ull * tb.denom / tb.numer;   /* device period, 10 ms */
+    next = mach_absolute_time() + tick;
     LOG_FN_CALL(9, "timer_loop");
     while (s->valid) {
-        usleep(10000); /* device period, 10 ms */
-        if (s->event && s->started)
+        mach_wait_until(next);
+        now = mach_absolute_time();
+        next += tick;
+        if (now > next + tick) next = now + tick;   /* after a stall, no burst */
+        if (!s->event || !s->started) continue;
+        padding = atomic_load_explicit(&s->write_pos, memory_order_acquire)
+                - atomic_load_explicit(&s->play_pos, memory_order_acquire);
+        if (padding < 4ull * period_frames || now - last >= period) {
             NtSetEvent(s->event, NULL);
+            last = now;
+        }
     }
 }
 
